<header class="page-hero">
  <div class="page-hero-inner">
    <span class="tag">the first question</span>
    <h1 class="page-h1">Who to pitch</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">
      A list of companies is easy to get and nearly worthless. What you need is a
      short list you can defend in a pipeline review — each one with the case for
      it, the case against it, a real person to write to, and the shortest warm
      path in. That is what a run gives you before it writes a word.
    </p>
    <div class="page-hero-actions">
      <a class="btn-cta" href="/#scan">Scan your site — free</a>
      <a class="btn-ghost" href="/what-to-send/">Then: what to send →</a>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="flow">
  <div class="flow-inner">

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">01</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>It works out who the company actually is — before anything else runs.</h2>
        <p>
          A target is identified by its website, not by its name. The studio opens
          the site and follows the pages that name an operator — footer, terms,
          privacy, about — reads what they sell and to whom, and only then starts
          searching. Everything downstream is handed that as settled fact.
        </p>
        <p>
          It reports back the pages it opened and the same-named businesses it
          <em>ruled out</em>. That second list is the point: "Northwind" is four
          companies, and a pitch researched against the wrong one is worse than no
          pitch at all.
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">You see the pages read and the companies ruled out, on every run.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">02</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>It scores fit against your brief — and argues both sides.</h2>
        <p>
          Your brief says what you are hunting for, in your own words. Every target
          is scored against that, and the score comes with a written fit thesis and
          the signals underneath it — the ones for and the ones against, side by
          side, each rated by how recent it is and how often it recurs.
        </p>
        <p>
          A tool that only ever says yes is a tool you stop reading. The signals
          against a company are what make a shortlist worth the name.
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">Fit thesis · signals for and against · recency and recurrence on each.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">03</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>Decision-makers, with sources. No source, no name.</h2>
        <p>
          A name appears only where a credible public source supports it, and the
          pitch links what was read. Every name is rated — <strong>confirmed</strong>
          when the company or the person states the role, <strong>likely</strong> when
          a secondary source does, <strong>unverified</strong> when it is inference.
          Where nothing backs a name, the role comes back blank for enrichment
          rather than filled with a guess.
        </p>
        <p>
          A LinkedIn profile is linked only where one was actually read. Where it
          was not, you get a search instead, drawn dashed, so it can never pass for
          a profile the studio found.
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">The confidence rating is about the name, not the person.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">04</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>Lookalikes, so one good target becomes a quarter's worth.</h2>
        <p>
          Every run ends with companies like the one you just researched. Tick the
          ones worth pursuing and the button counts them — <em>"Run 3 pitches"</em> —
          so a shortlist goes out in one action. A bracketed name is a gap in the
          research rather than a target, so it never gets a checkbox.
        </p>
        <p>
          Already have the list? Paste it. Up to a hundred companies at a time,
          researched and drafted while the tab is closed.
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">A run is a background job. Start it and leave — it finishes without you.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="flowstep">
      <span class="flowstep-n">05</span>
      <div class="flowstep-body">
        <h2>The warm path in, from the network you already have.</h2>
        <p>
          Upload your connections once and every target is checked against them.
          Where somebody you already know can open the door, you get the chain —
          you, the connector, the company — and who they are to you.
        </p>
        <p>
          Email addresses in a LinkedIn export are deleted at import, and your
          network never travels on a shared pitch.
        </p>
        <p class="flowstep-proof">And the intro request comes drafted — that half is on the next page.</p>
      </div>
    </article>

  </div>
</section>

